# Line-ending normalization.
# C# files — always LF in the index and the working tree on every platform. Without this, CRLF files
# that slipped into the index on Windows fail `dotnet format whitespace` on Linux (it expects LF)
# while the local run is green — a mismatch that is hard to diagnose.
*.cs text eol=lf
# Markdown files — also always LF in the index. The rule removes EOL noise from diffs and keeps
# `git blame` intact on files with mixed line endings. It applies to new and changed `.md` files;
# existing files were not renormalized in bulk when the rule was added.
*.md text eol=lf
# Shell scripts — always LF in both the index and the working tree. The rule mirrors the `*.cs` one
# but catches the opposite case: scripts are LF in the index anyway, yet a Windows checkout wrote
# them with CRLF and `docker build` failed with `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory`
# — invisible on Linux, where the working tree matches the index.
*.sh text eol=lf
# MDX documentation pages — always LF in the index and the working tree. Generated blocks in these
# pages are written with LF and committed, so a CRLF checkout would make the generator rewrite the
# page on every run and the consistency check fail between runs.
*.mdx text eol=lf
